parents of ccsd students are now  weighing in after  months of turning their homes into  classrooms eight news now reporter kate  houston spoke to two mothers to see how  they feel about this announcement  we received hundreds of comments from  parents on social media about the  announcement  there were many contrasting viewpoints  all pointing to what they think is the  best decision for their child  yereni estrada is a single mother of  five trying to balance distance learning  and her job for months she's had to  bring her kindergartner to her  office to assist him during school if i  leave him at home then they won't be  able to help him because they all have  different times in classes  now she's behind on her workload and  can't focus as much on her older kids at  home  estrada says she needs her five-year-old  back in the classroom  that's the group that is more hard on  the parents  because those are the kids that you have  to be paying attention that you have to  constantly be sitting with them  other parents are adamant about keeping  their kids at home  katie busser is a mom of five with two  kids that would be impacted by the  decision  it just didn't seem to make much sense  other than to get people's hopes up for  something  that i don't think will come to fruition  busser is concerned about the risks of  exposing her kids and school  staff to the virus especially to those  who are immunocompromised  it's not something i would wish on my  worst enemy i certainly wouldn't list it  on a child  and i think people who don't believe  that it can  affect children i think it's wishful  thinking on their parts  due to the wide variety of family  dynamics some say it's difficult to find  a one-size-fits-all solution  kate houston 8 news now
 

